- Add one extra feature to the meta-layer: disagreement flag `abs(p1 - p2) > 0`.
  - This lets the combiner behave differently when both models agree versus when they conflict.
- Use the meta-learner only as a post-prediction layer:
  - fit it on validation outputs from the two fixed base learners,
  - then apply the same two base learners to test data,
  - feed the two test outputs into the fitted meta-learner for final prediction.
- If you want an even safer version without extra fitting instability, use a rule-based fallback:
  - when both models agree, keep that prediction;
  - when they disagree, choose the prediction from the model whose validation output is farther from 0.5 on average over the validation set, i.e. the more confident model on this split.
- This is more effective than static averaging because it uses the models’ complementarity and confidence, not just their overall accuracies.
- It is also easy to implement, since it only adds a small combiner after both existing skrub pipelines have already produced predictions.
